 You can start by defining the variable with which you will perform your algebra. We will start by using the variable a to represent the number of additional action figures Henry needs to buy. Two things are known: Henry already has      3 action figures, and his collection will contain a total of      8 action figures. So he needs to buy a = 8 - 3 = >2< action figures. This is the first step in our algebraic expression. Additionally, each action figure costs $6. Therefore, the cost of buying a action figures is 6a . The total money he needs to finish his collection is then the expression \begin{align*}C = 6a = 6(8-3) = \boxed{30}Therefore,\end{align*} Html to represent the number of additional action figures Henry needs to buy. Two things are known: The total amount of money is then the second step in our equation, with 6 representing the cost per action figure and a representing the number of additional action figures he is buying. Therefore, the total amount of money is 6a . \begin{align*}C = 6a\end{align*} \begin{align*}a = 8 - 3 =5\end{align*}
\begin{align*}C = 6a = 6(8-3) =6\cdot5=\boxed{30}\end{align*} <p style="text-align: center;"><iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen="" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/v erwZq_yjMs" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9; width=100% height=100%" title="Can a quadrilatera be a parallelogram if two of its sides are not parallel? HY-polve Coloring answer Quiletin requiring Cas pointing Determinament.pdf"></iframe> </p>
The final answer is: <p style="text-align: center;"> \boxed{30} </p> Harry had 3 action figures but needed 8 total for a complete collection. He had 5 more to buy, and he had to buy 5 action figures to get to a total of 8. To find the answer, start by setting a equal to the number of action figures he needs to buy, which is 5. Next, create a plan by determining the cost to buy these 5 action figures. By setting a equal to 5, plug it into your chosen equation to find the answer. This is done by creating an expression with the cost per action figure and the action figures he has to buy. This is done by using six, because each action figure is six dollars. Create an equation with the cost per action figure and the number of action figures he has to buy. Then plug a (the number of action figures) into the equation. This is $6a. Finally, multiply the cost to buy the action figures by 5. This should be $6 * 5, equal to 30. Finally, to finish the equation, he needs $30. The final answer is 30. Source: Introduction to Engineering Problem Solving, 2nd Edition, by Rogers and Leung</p>
